Klaus Kassner <Klaus.Kassner@physik.uni-magdeburg.de> wrote:
>And then there is the old argument by Boudewijn.  The reason for the 
>wand to exist with 100% probability is that you may just not be able to 
>get an absolutely necessary item by chance, such as candles, or one that 
>is at least close to absolutely necessary, such as a ring of levitation 
>(or at least boots).  So you may need to burn one wish for something 
>"absolutely" necessary.  If you don't have more than one, where is the 
>reward for cleaning out the castle?

Levitation is only moderately close to necessary.  There are other ways
to get back across the Medusa, Castle, and Juiblex levels; waterwalking
(and there is a *guaranteed* pair of boots of water walking in Vlad's
tower), ice magic, and boulders all spring to mind.  On the way *down*
the dungeon, you can get past Medusa and Juiblex by means of digging
magic (or even a pick-axe).

And the castle already contains four storerooms and four dragons, which
are quite a reward in themselves.

David's proposal is that (a) wands of wishing should be unwrestable and
unrechargeable and (b) the Castle wand should *always* have three wishes
in it when found.

>And finally, I agree also with David's argument that the game should be 
>winnable without (too much) help by the RNG.  In fact, I tend to be 
>purist in that respect -- I believe that there should be essentially no 
>games that are lost due to the RNG.

This became an impossible desire when the ability for monsters to use
items was introduced.  Even a wand of fire or cold can kill a first
level character.

